window.SHOTLIST={"lessons":[{"w":10,"d":"01","title":"Lesson 10.01: Metric Units of Measurement","file":"math-lesson-4-10-01-metric-units.html","fileUrl":"../../optima-4th-math/math-lesson-4-10-01-metric-units.html","clips":[{"id":"think","label":"Teacher · Think · Amy's counter (about 1 min) · Warm-Up, under the Essential Question","path":"teacher/g4math-10-01-think","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Welcome to Metric Market! It's Market Day, and Amy wants to open her juice stand.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Market Day board]\n\nBut the Smudge mixed up the whole town. The Market Day board right under me has five jobs. Each one uses today's math.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Three planks]\n\nHere's the first puzzle. Mo is building Amy's counter out of three planks, end to end. Each plank is 1 meter 65 centimeters long. How long is the whole counter?\n\n[ON SCREEN: The big question]\n\nThat length has two units in it, meters and centimeters. So how do we add or multiply an amount like that? Don't wait for me to show you. Try it your way first, on the Think About It bar. Write down how you did it. On the Learn tab, the Math Crew will show you two ways, and you can see if one matches yours.","want":true},{"id":"learn","label":"Teacher · Learn · two ways on the mat (about 1½ min) · Learn, top (before the Math Crew)","path":"teacher/g4math-10-01-learn","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Look at Mo's planks. Each one is 1 meter 65 centimeters. What do you notice about that number? It has two units in it. That's what makes today different.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: The counter]\n\n[DRAW: trace around the whole bar, then tap the question mark.]\n\nThree planks, end to end. The whole bar is the counter. That's our question.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Live: The mat]\n\n[DO: build one plank: 1 meter, six 10s, five 1s. Tap + Add plank 2, then + Add plank 3. Then tap Trade 10 for 1 under the 1 cm column, and again under the 10 cm column.]\n\nOne plank on the mat: 1 meter, six tens, five ones. Two more planks. Look at the small columns: 15 ones and 18 tens. That's 3 meters and 195 centimeters, but 195 is more than a meter, so I trade. Ten ones for a ten. Ten tens for a meter. 4 meters 95 centimeters!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Two ways]\n\n[DRAW: fill in Atlas's blanks as you say them, then Felix's.]\n\nAtlas splits the units and regroups: 3 meters 195 centimeters becomes 4 meters 95. Felix changes to one unit first: 165 centimeters, times 3, is 495. Change back: 4 meters 95 centimeters. Same answer!\n\nPause and say out loud which way is more like yours. Then build the counter on your mat.","want":true},{"id":"do","label":"Teacher · Do · watch me solve (boards) (about 1½ min) · Learn, after the Market Measures Board","path":"teacher/g4math-10-01-do","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Now watch me solve three. Pause any time.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Problem 1]\n\n[DRAW: write 1 | 75 in row 1 and again in row 2. Add down: 2 | 150. Cross out 100 cm, write +1 m: 3 | 50.]\n\nTwo times 1 meter 75 centimeters. I write one plank, then the same plank again. Add the meters: 2. Add the centimeters: 150. 150 is more than 100, so I trade 100 centimeters for 1 meter. 3 meters 50 centimeters.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Problem 2]\n\nYour turn first. Pause the video and try this one on paper. Then press play and check.\n\n[DRAW: write 4 | 300 on top and 1 | 600 under it. Cross out 4, write 3; cross out 300, write 1,300. Subtract each column: 2 | 700.]\n\n4 kilograms 300 grams minus 1 kilogram 600 grams. 300 minus 600 won't work. So I regroup a kilogram: 3 kilograms 1,300 grams. 1,300 minus 600 is 700. 3 minus 1 is 2. 2 kilograms 700 grams. Felix checks it in grams: 4,300 minus 1,600 is 2,700. Same.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Spot the mistake]\n\n[DRAW: circle 83 g. Write 8 kg = 8,000 g, then 8,003 g.]\n\nIs this right? 8 kilograms is 8,000 grams. Plus 3 grams is 8,003. Not 83! The zeros hold the places. Watch for that on Millie's flour.","want":true},{"id":"wrap","label":"Teacher · Wrap · open the stand (about 45 s) · Show What You Know, after the Liter Well sign","path":"teacher/g4math-10-01-wrap","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Look how far you came!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two ways]\n\nToday you worked with amounts that have two units. You can split the units and regroup, or change it all to one unit first. Both give the same answer.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The sign]\n\nAnd that Liter Well sign? 1 liter is 1,000 milliliters, so 2 liters is 2,000, not 200. Press Play and fix it so Amy can open. In clean mode, you'll fix it right on the page.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nNext time we cross the Foot Bridge, where the trades are different: 12 inches make a foot. See you there, explorers!","want":false}],"cards":{"sci":{"think":[{"label":"Market Day board","tag":"TODAY'S REAL PROBLEM","title":"Help Amy open her juice stand","col":"#C56C3F","rows":[["Job 1","Stamp the measures"],["Job 2","Build Amy's counter"],["Job 3","Add up the juice orders"],["Job 4","Help Millie with her flour"],["Job 5","Fix the Liter Well sign"]]},{"label":"Three planks","tag":"THINK ABOUT IT","title":"3 planks, each 1 m 65 cm","col":"#6B3FA0","body":"Laid end to end, how long is the counter?"},{"label":"The big question","tag":"ESSENTIAL QUESTION","title":"How do you work with 1 m 65 cm?","col":"#2DB87A","body":"Two units in one amount. Try it your way first."}],"learn":[{"label":"Board: The counter","kind":"board","pic":"10-01-learn-counter","tag":"Amy's counter"},{"label":"Live: The mat","kind":"live","model":"counter","tag":"On your page","page":"math-lesson-4-10-01-metric-units.html"},{"label":"Board: Two ways","kind":"board","pic":"10-01-learn-twoways","tag":"Two ways"}],"do":[{"label":"Board: Problem 1","kind":"board","pic":"10-01-do-p1","tag":"Problem 1"},{"label":"Board: Problem 2","kind":"board","pic":"10-01-do-p2","tag":"Problem 2"},{"label":"Board: Spot the mistake","kind":"board","pic":"10-01-do-mistake","tag":"Spot the mistake"}],"wrap":[{"label":"Two ways","tag":"TODAY","title":"Split or one unit","col":"#2DB87A","body":"Both work. Regroup when the small unit gets too big."},{"label":"The sign","tag":"YOU PROVED IT","title":"2 L = 2,000 mL","col":"#C56C3F","body":"1 L = 1,000 mL. Not 100!"},{"label":"Next time","tag":"NEXT LESSON","title":"The Foot Bridge","col":"#6B3FA0","body":"Feet and inches: 12 inches make a foot."}]}},"words":[{"w":"millimeter (mm)","d":"smallest common length unit. 10 mm = 1 cm. About the thickness of a dime.","e":"📏"},{"w":"centimeter (cm)","d":"100 cm = 1 m. About the width of a fingernail.","e":"📐"},{"w":"meter (m)","d":"1,000 m = 1 km. About the height of a doorknob from the floor.","e":"📏"},{"w":"conversion","d":"changing one unit into another while keeping the same length, mass or capacity.","e":"🔄"}]},{"w":10,"d":"02","title":"Lesson 10.02: Customary Units of Length","file":"math-lesson-4-10-02-customary-length.html","fileUrl":"../../optima-4th-math/math-lesson-4-10-02-customary-length.html","clips":[{"id":"think","label":"Teacher · Think · Gus's two posts (about 1 min) · Warm-Up, under the Essential Question","path":"teacher/g4math-10-02-think","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Welcome to the Foot Bridge!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Bridge board]\n\nLast night the Smudge snapped Gus's rope rail, so nobody can cross to Eastside. The board right under me has six bridge jobs. Each one uses today's math.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two posts]\n\nHere's the first puzzle. Gus has two tall posts at the ends of his bridge. The north post is 6 feet 5 inches tall. The tag on the south post says 79 inches. Which post is taller, and by how many inches?\n\n[ON SCREEN: The big question]\n\nBefore you touch a single piece, look at the two heights. What do you notice? What do you wonder? Write that down first. Then build a foot on Gus's ruler, so you know how many inches make a foot, and try the post puzzle your own way. Hold on to your answer. On the Learn tab, the Math Crew will show you two ways. See if one matches yours.","want":true},{"id":"learn","label":"Teacher · Learn · two ways on the Rope Ruler (about 1½ min) · Learn, top (before the Math Crew)","path":"teacher/g4math-10-02-learn","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Look at the two heights one more time. One has feet and inches. One has only inches. That's the whole problem: we can't compare them until they're written the same way.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: The posts]\n\n[DRAW: point to the north post's row, 6 | 5. Write 79 in beside the empty row for the south post.]\n\nNorth post, 6 feet 5 inches. South post, 79 inches. We need them in the same unit.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Two ways]\n\n[DRAW: fill in Atlas's blanks as you say them, then Felix's.]\n\nAtlas changes the south post to feet and inches. He splits 79 with a number bond: 72 and 7. 72 inches is 6 feet. So the south post is 6 feet 7 inches, and 7 is 2 more than 5. Felix changes the north post to inches instead. 6 times 12 is 72, plus 5 is 77. 79 minus 77 is 2. Both ways say the south post, by 2 inches.\n\nPause here. Say out loud which way is more like what you did.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Live: The Rope Ruler]\n\n[DO: tap the 1 ft piece 3 times. Tap the 1 in piece 10 times. Point to the number sentence. Type 10 in the empty circle of the number bond and tap Check.]\n\nNow Rosa's rope, 46 inches. On the Rope Ruler I lay whole feet first: 12, 24, 36. One more foot would be 48. Too long! So I fill in with inches, up to 46. In the bond: 36 and 10. 3 feet 10 inches.\n\nRemember, 12 inches make a foot, not 10. Now build Rosa's rope on your page.","want":true},{"id":"do","label":"Teacher · Do · watch me solve (boards) (about 1½ min) · Learn, after the Foot Bridge Board","path":"teacher/g4math-10-02-do","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Now watch me solve three. Pause any time.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Problem 1]\n\n[DRAW: write 3 | 7 in row 1 and 1 | 8 in row 2. Add down: 4 | 15. Cross out 15, write 3, and add 1 to the feet: 5 | 3.]\n\n3 feet 7 inches plus 1 foot 8 inches. Feet: 4. Inches: 15. That's more than a foot, so I trade 12 inches for 1 foot. 5 feet 3 inches.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Problem 2]\n\nYour turn first. Pause the video and try this one on paper. Then press play and check.\n\n[DRAW: write 4 | 3 on top and 2 | 7 under it. Cross out 4, write 3. Cross out 3 in, write 15. Subtract each column: 1 | 8.]\n\n4 feet 3 inches minus 2 feet 7 inches. 3 minus 7 won't work. So I trade 1 foot for 12 inches: 3 feet 15 inches. 15 minus 7 is 8. 3 minus 2 is 1. 1 foot 8 inches. Felix checks it in inches: 51 minus 31 is 20, and 20 inches is 1 foot 8 inches. Same!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Problem 3]\n\n[DRAW: under 3 yd write 9 ft. Add the 2 ft: 11 ft. Write 11 × 12 = 132 in, then + 5 in = 137 in.]\n\n3 yards 2 feet 5 inches, all in inches. One step at a time. 3 yards is 9 feet. 9 and 2 more make 11 feet. Each foot is 12 inches, so 11 feet is 132 inches. Add the 5: 137 inches.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Spot the mistake]\n\n[DRAW: circle 3 ft 3 in. Write 13 in = 12 in + 1 in, then 3 ft 1 in.]\n\nIs this right? Someone treated inches like tens and ones. A foot is 12 inches, not 10. 13 inches is 12 and 1, so it's 3 feet 1 inch. Watch for that when you cut the rope!","want":true},{"id":"wrap","label":"Teacher · Wrap · open the bridge (about 45 s) · Show What You Know, after the last game window","path":"teacher/g4math-10-02-wrap","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Look how far you came!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two ways]\n\nToday you compared, added and subtracted lengths with feet and inches, and cut a rope on the Trade Mat. You can work in feet and inches and trade 12 inches for a foot, or change it all to inches first. Both give the same answer.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The banner]\n\nAnd the Smudge's banner? 2 feet 15 inches is 39 inches, but 3 feet 5 inches is 41 inches. They don't match! 15 inches is 1 foot and 3 inches, so the banner should say 3 feet 3 inches. And you chased the Smudge all the way to Eastside!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nNext time we walk into the General Store in Eastside, where 16 ounces make a pound. See you there, explorers!","want":false}],"cards":{"sci":{"think":[{"label":"Bridge board","tag":"TODAY'S REAL PROBLEM","title":"Fix the Foot Bridge rail","col":"#C56C3F","rows":[["Job 1","Build a foot"],["Job 2","Measure Rosa's rope"],["Job 3","Join the two ropes"],["Job 4","Cut Gus's spare rope"],["Job 5","Fill Eastside's rope order"],["Job 6","Prove the Smudge's banner wrong"]]},{"label":"Two posts","tag":"THINK ABOUT IT","title":"North post: 6 ft 5 in · South post: 79 in","col":"#6B3FA0","body":"Which post is taller, and by how many inches?"},{"label":"The big question","tag":"ESSENTIAL QUESTION","title":"Which is longer, 6 ft 5 in or 79 in? How can you be sure?","col":"#2DB87A","body":"Two units in one length. Notice, wonder, then try it your way."}],"learn":[{"label":"Board: The posts","kind":"board","pic":"10-02-learn-posts","tag":"Gus's two posts"},{"label":"Board: Two ways","kind":"board","pic":"10-02-learn-twoways","tag":"Two ways"},{"label":"Live: The Rope Ruler","kind":"live","model":"rope","tag":"On your page","page":"math-lesson-4-10-02-customary-length.html"}],"do":[{"label":"Board: Problem 1","kind":"board","pic":"10-02-do-p1","tag":"Problem 1"},{"label":"Board: Problem 2","kind":"board","pic":"10-02-do-p2","tag":"Problem 2"},{"label":"Board: Problem 3","kind":"board","pic":"10-02-do-p3","tag":"Problem 3"},{"label":"Board: Spot the mistake","kind":"board","pic":"10-02-do-mistake","tag":"Spot the mistake"}],"wrap":[{"label":"Two ways","tag":"TODAY","title":"Feet and inches, or all inches","col":"#2DB87A","body":"Both work. Trade 12 inches for a foot."},{"label":"The banner","tag":"YOU PROVED IT","title":"2 ft 15 in = 3 ft 3 in","col":"#C56C3F","body":"Not 3 ft 5 in. A foot is 12 inches, not 10!"},{"label":"Next time","tag":"NEXT LESSON","title":"The General Store","col":"#6B3FA0","body":"Pounds and ounces: 16 ounces make a pound."}]}},"words":[]}],"poems":[],"teacher":[],"teacherDocs":{"10-01":"# Lesson 10.01 teacher videos\n\n## Think · Amy's counter · g4math-10-01-think\n\nWelcome to Metric Market! It's Market Day, and Amy wants to open her juice stand.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Market Day board]\n\nBut the Smudge mixed up the whole town. The Market Day board right under me has five jobs. Each one uses today's math.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Three planks]\n\nHere's the first puzzle. Mo is building Amy's counter out of three planks, end to end. Each plank is 1 meter 65 centimeters long. How long is the whole counter?\n\n[ON SCREEN: The big question]\n\nThat length has two units in it, meters and centimeters. So how do we add or multiply an amount like that? Don't wait for me to show you. Try it your way first, on the Think About It bar. Write down how you did it. On the Learn tab, the Math Crew will show you two ways, and you can see if one matches yours.\n\n## Learn · two ways on the mat · g4math-10-01-learn\n\nLook at Mo's planks. Each one is 1 meter 65 centimeters. What do you notice about that number? It has two units in it. That's what makes today different.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: The counter]\n\n[DRAW: trace around the whole bar, then tap the question mark.]\n\nThree planks, end to end. The whole bar is the counter. That's our question.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Live: The mat]\n\n[DO: build one plank: 1 meter, six 10s, five 1s. Tap + Add plank 2, then + Add plank 3. Then tap Trade 10 for 1 under the 1 cm column, and again under the 10 cm column.]\n\nOne plank on the mat: 1 meter, six tens, five ones. Two more planks. Look at the small columns: 15 ones and 18 tens. That's 3 meters and 195 centimeters, but 195 is more than a meter, so I trade. Ten ones for a ten. Ten tens for a meter. 4 meters 95 centimeters!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Two ways]\n\n[DRAW: fill in Atlas's blanks as you say them, then Felix's.]\n\nAtlas splits the units and regroups: 3 meters 195 centimeters becomes 4 meters 95. Felix changes to one unit first: 165 centimeters, times 3, is 495. Change back: 4 meters 95 centimeters. Same answer!\n\nPause and say out loud which way is more like yours. Then build the counter on your mat.\n\n## Do · watch me solve (boards) · g4math-10-01-do\n\nNow watch me solve three. Pause any time.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Problem 1]\n\n[DRAW: write 1 | 75 in row 1 and again in row 2. Add down: 2 | 150. Cross out 100 cm, write +1 m: 3 | 50.]\n\nTwo times 1 meter 75 centimeters. I write one plank, then the same plank again. Add the meters: 2. Add the centimeters: 150. 150 is more than 100, so I trade 100 centimeters for 1 meter. 3 meters 50 centimeters.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Problem 2]\n\nYour turn first. Pause the video and try this one on paper. Then press play and check.\n\n[DRAW: write 4 | 300 on top and 1 | 600 under it. Cross out 4, write 3; cross out 300, write 1,300. Subtract each column: 2 | 700.]\n\n4 kilograms 300 grams minus 1 kilogram 600 grams. 300 minus 600 won't work. So I regroup a kilogram: 3 kilograms 1,300 grams. 1,300 minus 600 is 700. 3 minus 1 is 2. 2 kilograms 700 grams. Felix checks it in grams: 4,300 minus 1,600 is 2,700. Same.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Spot the mistake]\n\n[DRAW: circle 83 g. Write 8 kg = 8,000 g, then 8,003 g.]\n\nIs this right? 8 kilograms is 8,000 grams. Plus 3 grams is 8,003. Not 83! The zeros hold the places. Watch for that on Millie's flour.\n\n## Wrap · open the stand · g4math-10-01-wrap\n\nLook how far you came!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two ways]\n\nToday you worked with amounts that have two units. You can split the units and regroup, or change it all to one unit first. Both give the same answer.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The sign]\n\nAnd that Liter Well sign? 1 liter is 1,000 milliliters, so 2 liters is 2,000, not 200. Press Play and fix it so Amy can open. In clean mode, you'll fix it right on the page.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nNext time we cross the Foot Bridge, where the trades are different: 12 inches make a foot. See you there, explorers!\n","10-02":"# Lesson 10.02 teacher videos\n\n## Think · Gus's two posts · g4math-10-02-think\n\nWelcome to the Foot Bridge!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Bridge board]\n\nLast night the Smudge snapped Gus's rope rail, so nobody can cross to Eastside. The board right under me has six bridge jobs. Each one uses today's math.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two posts]\n\nHere's the first puzzle. Gus has two tall posts at the ends of his bridge. The north post is 6 feet 5 inches tall. The tag on the south post says 79 inches. Which post is taller, and by how many inches?\n\n[ON SCREEN: The big question]\n\nBefore you touch a single piece, look at the two heights. What do you notice? What do you wonder? Write that down first. Then build a foot on Gus's ruler, so you know how many inches make a foot, and try the post puzzle your own way. Hold on to your answer. On the Learn tab, the Math Crew will show you two ways. See if one matches yours.\n\n## Learn · two ways on the Rope Ruler · g4math-10-02-learn\n\nLook at the two heights one more time. One has feet and inches. One has only inches. That's the whole problem: we can't compare them until they're written the same way.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: The posts]\n\n[DRAW: point to the north post's row, 6 | 5. Write 79 in beside the empty row for the south post.]\n\nNorth post, 6 feet 5 inches. South post, 79 inches. We need them in the same unit.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Two ways]\n\n[DRAW: fill in Atlas's blanks as you say them, then Felix's.]\n\nAtlas changes the south post to feet and inches. He splits 79 with a number bond: 72 and 7. 72 inches is 6 feet. So the south post is 6 feet 7 inches, and 7 is 2 more than 5. Felix changes the north post to inches instead. 6 times 12 is 72, plus 5 is 77. 79 minus 77 is 2. Both ways say the south post, by 2 inches.\n\nPause here. Say out loud which way is more like what you did.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Live: The Rope Ruler]\n\n[DO: tap the 1 ft piece 3 times. Tap the 1 in piece 10 times. Point to the number sentence. Type 10 in the empty circle of the number bond and tap Check.]\n\nNow Rosa's rope, 46 inches. On the Rope Ruler I lay whole feet first: 12, 24, 36. One more foot would be 48. Too long! So I fill in with inches, up to 46. In the bond: 36 and 10. 3 feet 10 inches.\n\nRemember, 12 inches make a foot, not 10. Now build Rosa's rope on your page.\n\n## Do · watch me solve (boards) · g4math-10-02-do\n\nNow watch me solve three. Pause any time.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Problem 1]\n\n[DRAW: write 3 | 7 in row 1 and 1 | 8 in row 2. Add down: 4 | 15. Cross out 15, write 3, and add 1 to the feet: 5 | 3.]\n\n3 feet 7 inches plus 1 foot 8 inches. Feet: 4. Inches: 15. That's more than a foot, so I trade 12 inches for 1 foot. 5 feet 3 inches.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Problem 2]\n\nYour turn first. Pause the video and try this one on paper. Then press play and check.\n\n[DRAW: write 4 | 3 on top and 2 | 7 under it. Cross out 4, write 3. Cross out 3 in, write 15. Subtract each column: 1 | 8.]\n\n4 feet 3 inches minus 2 feet 7 inches. 3 minus 7 won't work. So I trade 1 foot for 12 inches: 3 feet 15 inches. 15 minus 7 is 8. 3 minus 2 is 1. 1 foot 8 inches. Felix checks it in inches: 51 minus 31 is 20, and 20 inches is 1 foot 8 inches. Same!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Problem 3]\n\n[DRAW: under 3 yd write 9 ft. Add the 2 ft: 11 ft. Write 11 × 12 = 132 in, then + 5 in = 137 in.]\n\n3 yards 2 feet 5 inches, all in inches. One step at a time. 3 yards is 9 feet. 9 and 2 more make 11 feet. Each foot is 12 inches, so 11 feet is 132 inches. Add the 5: 137 inches.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Board: Spot the mistake]\n\n[DRAW: circle 3 ft 3 in. Write 13 in = 12 in + 1 in, then 3 ft 1 in.]\n\nIs this right? Someone treated inches like tens and ones. A foot is 12 inches, not 10. 13 inches is 12 and 1, so it's 3 feet 1 inch. Watch for that when you cut the rope!\n\n## Wrap · open the bridge · g4math-10-02-wrap\n\nLook how far you came!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two ways]\n\nToday you compared, added and subtracted lengths with feet and inches, and cut a rope on the Trade Mat. You can work in feet and inches and trade 12 inches for a foot, or change it all to inches first. Both give the same answer.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The banner]\n\nAnd the Smudge's banner? 2 feet 15 inches is 39 inches, but 3 feet 5 inches is 41 inches. They don't match! 15 inches is 1 foot and 3 inches, so the banner should say 3 feet 3 inches. And you chased the Smudge all the way to Eastside!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nNext time we walk into the General Store in Eastside, where 16 ounces make a pound. See you there, explorers!\n"},"pics":{"10-01-learn-counter":"../../optima-4th-math/assets/video/boards/10-01/10-01-learn-counter.png?v=3f5b1abb","10-01-learn-twoways":"../../optima-4th-math/assets/video/boards/10-01/10-01-learn-twoways.png?v=0e78f43a","10-01-do-p1":"../../optima-4th-math/assets/video/boards/10-01/10-01-do-p1.png?v=ed4a8eec","10-01-do-p2":"../../optima-4th-math/assets/video/boards/10-01/10-01-do-p2.png?v=38271c21","10-01-do-mistake":"../../optima-4th-math/assets/video/boards/10-01/10-01-do-mistake.png?v=6108e498","10-02-learn-posts":"../../optima-4th-math/assets/video/boards/10-02/10-02-learn-posts.png?v=08d8833b","10-02-learn-twoways":"../../optima-4th-math/assets/video/boards/10-02/10-02-learn-twoways.png?v=32e115fa","10-02-do-p1":"../../optima-4th-math/assets/video/boards/10-02/10-02-do-p1.png?v=a01fd6a0","10-02-do-p2":"../../optima-4th-math/assets/video/boards/10-02/10-02-do-p2.png?v=d6f61255","10-02-do-p3":"../../optima-4th-math/assets/video/boards/10-02/10-02-do-p3.png?v=056d3da5","10-02-do-mistake":"../../optima-4th-math/assets/video/boards/10-02/10-02-do-mistake.png?v=4abc0d54"},"picCaps":{"10-01-learn-counter":"Board: The counter","10-01-learn-twoways":"Board: Two ways","10-01-do-p1":"Board: Problem 1","10-01-do-p2":"Board: Problem 2","10-01-do-mistake":"Board: Spot the mistake","10-02-learn-posts":"Board: The posts","10-02-learn-twoways":"Board: Two ways","10-02-do-p1":"Board: Problem 1","10-02-do-p2":"Board: Problem 2","10-02-do-p3":"Board: Problem 3","10-02-do-mistake":"Board: Spot the mistake"}};
